# Trivy misconfiguration baseline for terraform/.
#
# DELIBERATELY EMPTY OF IDs. Every accepted finding is suppressed with an
# inline `#trivy:ignore:AVD-AWS-xxxx` marker directly above the resource or
# attribute it applies to, next to the sentence explaining why.
#
# Why not list the IDs here: trivy resolves --ignorefile relative to the
# process working directory, not to the scan target. Both callers scan this
# tree from the repo root --
#
#     .github/workflows/security.yml   trivy-action, scan-ref: terraform
#     Justfile                         scan-tf: trivy config ... terraform
#
# -- so `terraform/.trivyignore` was never loaded by either one, and the
# entries that used to live here silently did nothing: `just scan-tf` reported
# 2 CRITICAL + 7 HIGH accepted findings on every run, which is exactly how a
# genuinely new finding gets lost. Inline markers are path-independent and
# work from either working directory; modules/, bootstrap/ and monitoring.tf
# already used them.
#
# Accepted findings and where their marker lives:
#   AVD-AWS-0053  internet-facing ALB (by design, WAF in front)   alb.tf, modules/*
#   AVD-AWS-0054  plain-HTTP listener for certificate-less stacks alb.tf, modules/ecs-stack
#   AVD-AWS-0095  unencrypted alerts topic (alarms cannot publish
#                 through the AWS-managed SNS key)                monitoring.tf
#   AVD-AWS-0104  unrestricted egress (ALB to dynamic task ENIs;
#                 tasks to Horizon/RPC/webhooks on 443)           security_groups.tf, modules/*
#   AVD-AWS-0132  S3 without a customer-managed key               s3.tf, bootstrap/, modules/*
#   AVD-AWS-0136  SNS jobs topic on alias/aws/sns                 sns_sqs.tf, modules/ecs-stack
#   AVD-AWS-0164  auto-assign public IP on public subnets         vpc.tf, modules/*
#
# Adding a new acceptance: put the marker and its justification on the
# resource. Do not re-add IDs to this file - they would not be read.
